What is static in C#?
Static in C# is a keyword used to declare a member of a class that belongs to the class itself rather than to any instance of the class. Static members are shared among all instances of the class, and they can be accessed without creating an instance of the class.
In C#, the static keyword can be applied to classes, methods, fields, properties, events, and even constructors. When a class is declared static, it means that it cannot be instantiated and all its members must also be static. This is useful for utility classes that provide helper methods or constants that don't require any instance-specific data.
For example, consider a class called MathUtilities that contains a static method called Add:
public static class MathUtilities
{
    public static int Add(int a, int b)
    {
        return a + b;
    }
}
In this case, the MathUtilities class is declared as static, and the Add method is also static. This means that you can call the Add method without creating an instance of the MathUtilities class, like this:
int result = MathUtilities.Add(2, 3);
Static methods can also access static fields and properties, but they cannot access instance members because they don't have a reference to a specific instance of the class. This makes static members useful for operations that don't depend on the state of an object

